A||||6||BAM-BAM|| How to?||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||05-02-2000||11:35 PM||||  I am finally getting ready to install my new poly bushings and was wondering what the trick is to removing the old ones(rubber). I believe someone once said that it is neccessary to leave the original metal sleeve to receive the new poly. Just want to know what tips are out there from those who have done this. TIA  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> Thanx, BAM-BAM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||05-03-2000||12:02 AM||||It depends what bushings you bought or are getting. If you have the Prothane brand from Suspension Restoration (which has the best price), you need to leave the outer metal casing in the control arm. The most common removal method, and the one I used, is to burn them out with a torch (preferably outdoors and up-wind).||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||05-03-2000||12:06 AM||||Try this: take a drill and a bit (about 3/16) and stick the bit between the sleeve and bushing. Turn the drill on, but not too fast and let the bit roll around the sleeve. Now grab the sleeve with pliers and pull it out. Now stick the bit between the bushing and whatever it's in  and do the same thing. Now hit the bushing with a hammer (not very hard) and tap it out.<P>Edit: after reading batboy's post I thought of something. I haven't done a control arm, just trailing arms used in 88s, so the casing my cause my method to not work. But it might.<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 05-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||05-03-2000||12:35 AM||||We had a thread on this not long ago...I burnt the upper and lower control arms out and replaced with Prothane bushings. <B>And it sucked!</B> Burning rubber...mmm...mmmmmmm.<P>Now someone posted in the thread a brilliant but not necessarily accesible method. He froze his old bushings while still in place in liquid nitrogen. Smacked 'em with a hammer and <I>PRESTO</I>! Shattered into pieces and fell on the floor. Now, not everyone has access to liquid nitrogen and for good reason. The stuff is dangerous. However, ogre suggested dry ice might get the rubber down almost as cold and allow for an easier removal.<P>Just a thought.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-03-2000||01:13 AM||||I didn't think the torch method was all that bad. Actually not bad at all and fairly easy. Once you get it flaming good and it's nice and hot, I used a large blunt screwdriver to push the middle sleeve out, let it flame for a few minutes more, then push the rubber out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-03-2000||01:16 AM||||I am the one that used Liquid Nitrogen. If you have access to some it takes about 10 minutes to do. 5 minutes of soaking and 5 minutes to tap them all out. The other thing you can do is go to a "paintless" dent repair place with a thermos. They use LN2 to remove small dings in cars. (They heat the metal up and pour LN2 on it which pulls the ding out of metal bodied cars) You may be able to get some from them. Caution LN2 will give you a nasty frostbite if you get it on your skin USE GLOVES. Also tap the bushing to shatter it. If you hit the metal sleeve it will also crack! It will take about 20 minutes for the sleeves to warm back up. If you drop one it will shatter like glass. Don't try to heat it up while frozen it will warp the sleeve.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||05-03-2000||12:36 PM||||Tried the burning method... way too messy for me.  I used a drill on the rear control arms and the foward cradle bushings.  It took about half the time and was ohh so much cleaner.  The liquid nitrogen thig sounds interesting, but I think I would end up buying new control arms anyway if I tried that.||||reg||1
